 ## Overview
-A spatial index structure that augments a branch-on-need octree with links to allow direct traversal to adjacent nodes, used to accelerate ray traversal in volume rendering [S2]. It differs from Zhou et al. [2010] by storing different payload (mesh vs point cloud) and maintaining only x-neighbor pointers instead of pointers to all 26 neighbors [S1].
+This method augments a branch-on-need octree with explicit links to adjacent nodes, enabling direct traversal to neighboring spatial regions without backtracking through the parent hierarchy [S2]. It solves the latency penalty of recursive ascent in sparse octrees by allowing rays to jump horizontally across node boundaries [S1]. Unlike structures that maintain full 26-neighbor connectivity, this variant typically restricts pointers to the X-axis (or specific face directions) to minimize memory overhead, distinguishing it from comprehensive adjacency graphs [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A view-dependent branch-on-need octree.
-**Outputs:** A 3D texture acting as a spatial index, encoded with neighbor pointers.
+The method constructs a spatial index on-the-fly from a view-dependent octree and encodes it into a 3D texture for GPU traversal [S2].
 
-**Data Structures:**
-The spatial index is encoded into an 8-bit RGBA 3D texture [S2]. Each octree node is encoded in 8 consecutive texels arranged in the x-direction [S2]. The layout uses the following tagging scheme in the Alpha (A) component:
-*   $A=1.0$: Inner node.
-*   $A=0.5$: Data node (leaf).
-*   $A=0.0$: Empty node.
